</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/942b5ac7697c72b9e3bc1e3b340daf7189fdf61f" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.671ex; width:2.361ex; height:2.509ex;" alt="{\displaystyle T_{s}}"/></span> is the temperature of the gas at the elevation of Earth's surface. Santiago and Visser remark that "gravity is the only force capable of creating temperature gradients in thermal equilibrium states without violating the laws of thermodynamics" and "the existence of Tolman's temperature gradient is not at all controversial (at least not within the general relativity community)."<sup about="#mwt168" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-SantiagoVisser19_30-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"SantiagoVisser19"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-SantiagoVisser19-30"}}'><a href="#cite_note-SantiagoVisser19-30" id="mwAbY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbg">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt171" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tolman1930_31-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tolman1930"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tolman1930-31"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tolman1930-31" id="mwAbo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbw">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

